namedtuple is a factory function for creating simple data classes. It creates classes with fields that are accessible by attribute lookup as well as being indices into a sequence class by field position, just like regular classes.  but namedtuple provides additional functionality to construct and access named tuples using the index (just like regular classes) as well as by name (just like dictionaries). This is done automatically using the values in the fields argument, which is a sequence or dict of field names. The resulting class is an ordinary new-style class. namedtuple provides some useful features including a backport of useful functionality from collections, added in Python 2.6.0: _replace(), which returns a copy of the tuple with specified field values replaced, and _asdict(), which returns a collections.namedtuple._asdict class instance (a subclass of collections.OrderedDict) from which user-defined named tuples can iterate.

Note: namedtuple is available in Python 2.6.0 and later. In earlier versions of Python, implementing aggregate-like classes with mutable data instead of a single string or list of items, or with item names that cannot be used in a variable name, requires using custom classes that implement specifications such as __getitem__ and __iter__.

